Tier 7 -- simulated cluster life, seeded (reaper guest, shape A).

A generator dealing weighted events from a fixed seed against a shadow model of
expected placement and lineage, with five invariants diffed continuously. The
oracle self-test is written before the generator: invariants fed hand-built
broken states must each complain, and that runs at tier-4 cost with no cluster
at all. See TESTING.md §7 and tests/cluster/sim/DESIGN.md.

    t_sim.sh    the tier entry: the oracle self-test FIRST (it refuses to spend
                a session on a checker that cannot fire), then every seed in
                tests/cluster/sim/seeds.txt, then -- opt-in -- hunting.

    shrink-real.sh
                OPT-IN, and deliberately not named t_*.sh so that no session
                pays for it by accident. It injects a real defect (the
                promotion-without-fencing patch of the rediscovery battery)
                into a scratch copy, runs the seed and window the tier-7
                rediscovery rows use, and asserts what tests/tier1/t_sim_shrink.sh
                cannot: that the SHRINKER is useful on a real failure -- the
                trace gets shorter, the reduction still fails on a cluster it
                has never seen, one step less passes, and the events left in it
                are the ones the failure is about. Half an hour or so, because
                every rerun rebuilds a three-node cluster -- and it raises
                SEANCE_SIM_SHRINK_BUDGET, because the default 1800 s is a
                battery's budget and refuses a reduction this file exists to
                make.

                    env REAPER_STATE=/tank/state sh tests/tier7/shrink-real.sh

Everything it drives lives in tests/cluster/sim/:

    run.sh          one seed -> one trace -> one verdict, against a cluster it
                    builds and destroys
    gen.subr        the seeded PRNG and the weighted chooser
    model.subr      the shadow model: what may happen next, and what must be
                    true
    world.subr      the real world driver: events applied to three vnet jails
                    and to real seance, and the observed state written out
    invariants.subr the checker (also fed by the tier-4 oracle self-test)
    oracle.subr     the cheap oracle around every seance invocation
    shrink.subr     prefix bisection, then event-kind removal
    seeds.txt       the committed battery

Environment:

    SEANCE_SIM_STEPS    events per seed (default 60)
    SEANCE_SIM_N        nodes (3; 4 for the even-N profile)
    SEANCE_SIM_SEEDS    a seed list that REPLACES the committed battery. For
                        the rediscovery battery, which asserts a failure and
                        needs to see it once. Announced in the output, so a run
                        that used it cannot be read as the battery.
    SEANCE_HUNT=1       draw fresh seeds until SEANCE_HUNT_BUDGET seconds are
                        gone (`reaper run --profile hunt`)
    SEANCE_HUNT_ONLY=1  with SEANCE_HUNT=1: skip the committed battery and hunt
                        straight away (`reaper run --profile huntonly`). The
                        battery is 2 h 45 m, so a hunt behind it cannot be
                        reached inside a session meant for hunting (D-162).
                        The ORACLE SELF-TEST still runs -- a hunt with a broken
                        oracle is noise -- and the output says in its own
                        assertion text that the battery DID NOT RUN, so a green
                        hunt-only stage can never be read as the battery.
                        Nominations are unchanged: a hunted seed that fails
                        with exit 1 is nominated, one that was killed (exit 3)
                        is not, and a run driven by SEANCE_SIM_SEEDS nominates
                        nothing. Without SEANCE_HUNT=1 it is a contract error,
                        not a guess. Every one of these decisions lives in
                        sim/hunt.subr and is tested by
                        tests/tier1/t_sim_hunt.sh, on a workstation.
    SEANCE_SIM_DRY=1    run.sh only: draw and model a trace with NO cluster,
                        which is how the generator and the model's
                        applicability rules are exercised on a workstation
                        (tests/tier1/t_sim_model.sh is the unit test)
    SEANCE_SIM_SHRINK_BUDGET
                        seconds a failing trace's reduction may cost (1800).
                        Every rerun rebuilds a cluster, so the reduction is
                        estimated before it is started and the UNREDUCED trace
                        is printed when it does not fit -- a shrink nobody
                        budgeted for can eat the session the battery was meant
                        to run in. The verdict is the same either way.
    SEANCE_SIM_NO_SHRINK=1
                        do not reduce at all (the shrinker's own reruns set it,
                        and so does the rediscovery battery)

A seed that finds a defect is written to $REAPER_OUT/sim/seeds-to-promote.txt.
It is NOT appended to seeds.txt automatically: this same runner is what the
rediscovery battery drives with a protection deliberately reverted, and a
runner that promoted on every failure would fill the permanent battery with
seeds that found a hole somebody made on purpose. Appending is the fixer's,
with a comment naming the defect (D-133).

Files named t_*.sh are tests; anything else here is ignored.
